---
import GuideLayout from '../../layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
---

<GuideLayout slug="block-youtube-ads-free">
  <div class="gx-guide">
    <div class="gx-hero">
      <p class="gx-hero__kicker">Still free in 2026</p>
      <p class="gx-hero__line">
        You can still block YouTube ads for <strong>free</strong>. Easiest: a blocking browser like
        <strong>Brave</strong>. Most reliable on desktop: a real filter-list extension. On the phone app,
        just watch in a browser.
      </p>
      <div class="gx-glance">
        <span class="gx-chip"><span class="gx-chip__i">🆓</span> $0</span>
        <span class="gx-chip"><span class="gx-chip__i">🖥</span> Desktop = best</span>
        <span class="gx-chip"><span class="gx-chip__i">📱</span> Mobile asterisk</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p class="gx-intro">
      YouTube's ad load keeps climbing and the "anti-adblock" pop-ups keep getting pushier. Good news:
      blocking YouTube ads on desktop is still free and still works in 2026. You just need the right tool
      and realistic expectations — here are the honest options, ranked.
    </p>

    <h2>🥇 The two routes that work</h2>
    <div class="gx-steps">
      <div class="gx-step">
        <div class="gx-step__num">1</div>
        <div class="gx-step__body">
          <h3>A blocking browser (easiest)</h3>
          <p>The lowest-effort route is a browser that blocks ads on its own — <strong>Brave</strong> being
            the obvious pick. Nothing to install, ads gone out of the box, works on phones too.</p>
          <p class="gx-note">⚠️ Trade-off: you're switching browsers, and built-in blockers sometimes lag a day or two behind YouTube's changes.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="gx-step">
        <div class="gx-step__num">2</div>
        <div class="gx-step__body">
          <h3>A real content-blocker extension (most reliable)</h3>
          <p>On Chrome, Brave, Edge, or Firefox, a proper blocker is the most reliable desktop route — it
            strips the ad slots before they play and hides the "ad break" UI entirely. That's exactly why we
            built <strong>AdBlock-Maxxer</strong>: EasyList compiled to native rules, YouTube ads stripped,
            Spotify ads muted. No account, no telemetry, no paid allowlist.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="gx-callout">
      <p class="gx-callout__tag">🎯 Two things that matter when you pick one</p>
      <ul class="gx-mini">
        <li><strong>It should use filter lists (EasyList)</strong> — not a tiny hand-made list that goes stale.</li>
        <li><strong>It shouldn't sell an "acceptable ads" allowlist.</strong> If a blocker takes money to let some ads through, you're not the customer — you're the product.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="gx-callout gx-callout--cyan">
      <p class="gx-callout__tag">📱 The mobile asterisk</p>
      <p>
        Desktop extensions don't run on iPhone Safari or inside the YouTube app. On your phone, the free
        options are a blocking browser (Brave) or, on iPhone, a browser like Orion that supports extensions.
        The YouTube app itself can't be extended — there's no free, no-jailbreak way to kill in-app ads that
        lasts. Watch in a browser instead.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2>🛡 What about "anti-adblock" warnings?</h2>
    <p>
      When YouTube flashes "ad blockers aren't allowed," it's <em>detecting</em> your blocker — not beating
      it. A maintained filter list updates to hide that prompt within days. If yours doesn't, it's not being
      maintained — switch.
    </p>

    <p class="gx-outro">
      <strong>Rule of thumb:</strong> free is fine, but never install a YouTube "ad blocker" that won't tell
      you its filter lists and how it makes money. That's how people end up with adware.
    </p>
  </div>
</GuideLayout>
